<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail Lomonosov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Mikhail Vasilyevich Lomonosov, Lomonosov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail Vasilyevich Lomonosov was a Russian polymath, scientist and writer, born in the village of Mishaninskaya in the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the son of a peasant fisherman turned ship owner. In 1730 he walked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and entered the Slavic Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Academy, and from 1736 he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-marburg">University of Marburg</a> under Christian Wolff and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a>. Among his discoveries were the atmosphere of Venus and the law of conservation of mass in chemical reactions, and he worked in chemistry, physics, mineralogy, history and philology. In 1755 he joined Count Ivan Shuvalov in founding Moscow University and, as a poet, shaped the modern Russian literary language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-lomonosov.jpg" alt="Mikhail Lomonosov" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Georg Caspar Prenner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M.V._Lomonosov_by_L.Miropolskiy_after_G.C.Prenner_(1787,_RAN).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lomonosov was born on 19 November 1711 (8 November in the Old Style) on an island in the Northern Dvina near Kholmogory, the son of Vasily Dorofeyevich Lomonosov, a prosperous peasant fisherman turned ship owner. A neighbour taught him to read, and at fourteen he was given a grammar by Meletius Smotrytsky and Leonty Magnitsky's Arithmetic. In 1730, aged 19 and determined to study sciences, he walked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and entered the Slavic Greek Latin Academy by falsely claiming to be a nobleman's son. He completed a twelve-year course in five years, and in 1736 won a scholarship to the St Petersburg Academy, which sent him abroad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-marburg">University of Marburg</a>, from November 1736 to July 1739, he studied under Christian Wolff, whose influence on him was the most lasting of his life, and in 1739 and 1740 he studied mineralogy and metallurgy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in June 1741. In 1745 he became a full member of the academy and its first Russian professor of chemistry, and he set up its first chemistry laboratory. In 1755 he joined Count Ivan Shuvalov in founding Moscow University.</p><p>In 1756 he repeated an experiment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-boyle">Robert Boyle</a> and concluded that the phlogiston theory was false, since the mass of a burnt metal in a sealed vessel stayed the same. During the transit of Venus in 1761 he inferred an atmosphere around the planet from an arc of light at ingress and egress. In July 1754 he demonstrated a model <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> with a coaxial rotor, and he argued that icebergs of the Southern Ocean could form only on land, which predicted Antarctica. His book On the Strata of the Earth appeared in 1763.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1764 Lomonosov was appointed State Councillor, rank V in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-of-ranks">Table of Ranks</a>. He died on 15 April 1765 (4 April in the Old Style) in St Petersburg. He is widely regarded as the Father of Russian Science, though many of his accomplishments were little known outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> until the late 19th and the 20th centuries. Moscow State University was renamed after him in 1940.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mikhail Lomonosov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1755 <b>Mikhail Lomonosov</b> defined three styles for Russian, from a high one full of Church Slavonic to a low one close to speech; the middle prevailed, and Russian keeps a whole layer of Church Slavonic words, usually higher and more abstract than their native twins, as poetic…</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Empress Elizabeth signed the decree founding Moscow University, proposed by Ivan Shuvalov and <b>Mikhail Lomonosov</b> (12 January Old Style).</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-12">12 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
